Thank you for the follow-up! Let me provide an example related to logging user profile data, where accessing the log level within IDestructuringPolicy would be highly beneficial.

Suppose we want to log all details of a user profile when the log level is Verbose, but for all other levels, we only want to log the username and email.

#
```c#
public class UserProfileDestructuringPolicy : IDestructuringPolicy
{
    public bool TryDestructure(object value, ILogEventPropertyValueFactory propertyValueFactory, out LogEventPropertyValue result)
    {
        if (value is UserProfile userProfile)
        {
            // Access the current log level (desired feature)
            var logLevel = // Ideally, we would obtain the current log level here

            // Log everything if the level is Verbose, otherwise just username and email
            if (logLevel == LogEventLevel.Verbose)
            {
                result = propertyValueFactory.CreatePropertyValue(new
                {
                    userProfile.UserName,
                    userProfile.Email,
                    userProfile.PhoneNumber,
                    userProfile.Address,
                    userProfile.DateOfBirth,
                    userProfile.AccountCreationDate,
                    // any other relevant fields
                }, true);
            }
            else
            {
                result = propertyValueFactory.CreatePropertyValue(new
                {
                    userProfile.UserName,
                    userProfile.Email
                }, true);
            }
            return true;
        }

        result = null;
        return false;
    }
}
``` 

In this scenario, being able to check the log level inside the destructuring policy would allow us to centralize the logic for what gets logged. Without this capability, we would need to implement conditional logic at every logging point or handle this in a more convoluted way, which can quickly become cumbersome in a large application.

Allowing access to the log level directly within IDestructuringPolicy would streamline the process, reducing code duplication and making the logging logic cleaner and more maintainable.

We have two options which require no breaking change to the exising IDestructuringPolicy interface

1. Introduce an RequiresContextAttribute attribute
```c#
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class)]
public class RequiresContextAttribute : Attribute
{
}
``` 
2. Introduce a marker interface IContextualDestructuringPolicy
```c#
public interface IContextualDestructuringPolicy : IDestructuringPolicy
{
    // No members, just a marker interface
}
``` 

If a policy has the attribute applied (or derived from the marker interface), we could wrap the value with the DestructuringContext below, othewise we could pass the original value as usual: 
```c#
public class DestructuringContext
{
    public LogEventLevel LogLevel { get; }
    public object Value { get; }

    public DestructuringContext(LogEventLevel logLevel, object value)
    {
        LogLevel = logLevel;
        Value = value;
    }
}
```

