Hi Raymond, thanks for dropping us a line!

There are a few subtleties and consistency issues around this that make it unappealing to implement in Serilog itself; there are a couple of options you might consider instead (in addition to just ignoring the existence of the cache).

Is your code using Serilog directly, or is it using a legacy logging API that's adapted onto Serilog?

If it's the former, using Serilog directly, then your best bet is to use SerilogAnalyzer to spot literal strings in log statements, and incrementally fix the code. Serilog has always had the API it does now - arguments to `Log` are message templates, and if this is ignored, Serilog can produce unexpected output.

E.g.

```csharp
var name= "{{Raymond}}";
Log.Information($"Hello, {name}");
// {{ and }} are escape sequences, so they're collapsed:
// [19:05:38 INF] Hello, {Raymond}
```

Besides, fixing the code will be the most enjoyable, productive, and beneficial option in the long run. Structured logging with full message templates is just a wonderful place to be! 🌞

Otherwise, if you _are_ using Serilog but can't allocate time to fix the code, creating a wrapper around Serilog's `ILogger` and implementing your own message template caching in there would give you pretty good bang for the buck. If you set `Log.Logger` to your own wrapper around Serilog you can reuse Serilog's template parsing and caching however you wish, and then forward events on to a wrapped `Logger` via its (explicit) implementation of `ILogEventSink.Emit()`.

If you're _not_ using Serilog directly, then your best option is to change your adapter to treat `log.Information("X")` as if it's `log.Information("{Message}", "X")` - or to do something with `MessageTemplateParser` and `ILogEventSink.Emit()` as suggested above, before forwarding to Serilog.

Hope this helps!
Nick

Yes, if configured mindfully, Serilog completely overcomes this issue.

However, this is formatter/sink-specific and relies on using Serilog as a structured logger (writing to unstructured output streams can definitely thwart this).

If you take the example from the OWASP article:

```
    var val = request["val"];
    try {
        int value = Int32.Parse(val);
    }
    catch (Exception ex) {
        log.Information("Failed to parse val {Val}");
    }
```

Then the resulting Serilog event in CLEF format will look like:

```json
{"@t":"...","@mt":"Failed to parse val {Val}","Val":"twenty-one"}
```

Imagining the "bad guy" scenario:

> if an attacker submits the string “twenty-one%0a%0aINFO:+User+logged+out%3dbadguy” ...

Serilog will produce a structured event like:

```json
{"@t":"...","@mt":"Failed to parse val {Val}","Val":"twenty-one\n\nINFO: User logged out=badguy"}
```

Note that the injected "event" is completely within the `Val` field and can't be mistaken for a "real" event.

This holds even if the injected event is made to look like JSON, as Serilog's JSON formatter escapes JSON values correctly, and so the fake event will still be entirely within the "Val" field.

Even when misusing the Seriog API and not correctly recording `Val` as structured data, injected content will still only ever appear within the message field or whatever field it's placed into, and can't masquerade as an entirely separate event.

Just to stress it again, you'll need to check how your formatter and sink behave, to be sure. I'm talking about the formatters in _Serilog.Formatting.Compact_ here.

