This isn't really the usage pattern that was originally envisaged for `Destructure.ByTransforming()`, which was added as a shortcut for a "real" `IDestructuringPolicy`, though it really does seem like it should work.

The return value of the delegate will still always need processing because the value has to end up as one of Serilog's internal event property objects (`ScalarValue` and so-on).

I'm not sure what a solution to this would look like that doesn't add more allocation overhead to the pipeline; some investigation needed.

I think you can work around this in an implementation of `IDestructuringPolicy`, by returning `false` if the credentials are "already log-safe":

```csharp
class CredentialDestructuringPolicy : IDestructuringPolicy
{
    public bool TryDestructure(object value, ILogEventPropertyValueFactory propertyValueFactory, out LogEventPropertyValue result)
    {
        if (value is Credentials c && !c.IsLogSafe())
        {
            // Recursive, but the check above will cause termination
            result = propertyValueFactory.CreatePropertyValue(c.LogSafe(), true);
            return true;
        }
        result = null;
        return false; // Let the default policy handle it
    }
}
```

