Adding a parameter to a method won't break source code calling that method if the parameter is given a default value:

```csharp
// Parameter `compact` added in a new version
void Seq(bool compact = false) {}

// Caller compiles unchanged - default value is used
Seq();
```

Behind the scenes though, the compiler is actually inserting the default values at the call sites:

```csharp
// What the method call above is actually compiled as
Seq(false);
```

This is required because when the CLR loads up the assemblies, the method signatures need to match exactly. Default arguments are just compile-time sugar: if one assembly was compiled against an older, parameterless, version of the method, the signatures won't match.

Hence, adding the parameter breaks existing _binaries_, even though it doesn't break existing source code. Thus "binary-breaking change" vs. "source-breaking change". Hope this helps - there should be some better information out there on the topic but this is the gist of it :-)

Cheers!
Nick

