Yes, Serilog can handle this. The main consideration you'll have is where to send the log events - none of the existing sinks will do a very good job of logging images (though the recipe below should keep things sane).

Assumptions:

 * Your images are wrapped up in a type distinct from `byte[]`; example below
 * The image data is immutable once logged

First, the type that carries the image data should be something like:

```csharp
class Image
{
    public string Type { get; private set; }
    public byte[] Data { get; private set; }

    public Image(string type, byte[] data)
    {
        Type = type;
        Data = data;
    }

    public override string ToString() => $"{Type} ({Data.Length} bytes)";
}
```

Then, mark the type as scalar - this means the logging pipeline will pass it through unchanged:

```csharp
Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .Destructure.AsScalar<Image>()
    // ...
```

This will now log your image:

```csharp
var input = new Image(...);
Log.Information("Processed {Input}", image);
```

The existing sinks won't know what to do with an `Image`, so they'll log it out as a string:

```
Processed PNG (12345 bytes)
```

You can then plug in a sink that understands what to do with image properties. Where are you sending the data currently?

Most of the log storage I'm aware of is strongly text-oriented. You might be better off writing a sink that uploads the images to some kind of shared file storage (a disk drive, SAN, blob store etc.) and then setting things up so that the other non-image-supporting sinks get a hyperlink or key to the data.

E.g.:

```
class Image
{
    public string Type { get; private set; }
    public byte[] Data { get; private set; }
    public string Key { get; } => $"image-{Guid.NewGuid():n}";

    public Image(string type, byte[] data)
    {
        Type = type;
        Data = data;
    }

    public override string ToString() => $"{Key} ({Type}, {Data.Length} bytes)";
}
```

