To add more details you can use middleware to either enrich events, or log each request with additional information.

To do either you'll need some middleware (`app.UseMiddleware<RequestLogger>()`), and:

``` csharp
public class RequestLogger
{
    readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public RequestLogger(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    public async Task Invoke(HttpContext context)
    {
        // E.g.:
        Log.Information("Received headers {@Headers}", context.Request.Headers);
        await _next.Invoke(context);
    }
}
```

Or to add properties:

``` csharp
public class RequestLogger
{
    readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public RequestLogger(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    public async Task Invoke(HttpContext context)
    {
        using (LogContext.PushProperty("Headers", context.Request.Headers, destructureObjects: true))
        {
            await _next.Invoke(context);
        }
    }
}
```

Using the latter approach can add a lot of weight to events, so I'd tend towards logging out just the one event if that suits what you're trying to achieve.

(The logging you get out of the box is from ASP.NET, so raising an issue in one of the ASP.NET projects about the missing information may achieve a better long-term outcome.)

Hope this helps!

