For this particular task, it's often easier to plug in an `ILogEventEnricher`:

``` csharp
    .Enrich.With(new BlacklistedPropertyRemover())
```

Where `BlacklistedPropertyRemover` just checks each event, and if-and-only-if the event contains a blacklisted property, reconstructs the event's `Properties` with the problematic ones removed:

(Pseudocode :-) )

``` csharp
class BlacklistedPropertyRemover : ILogEventEnricher
{
  public void Enrich(LogEvent logEvent, ...)
  {
    foreach (var prop in logEvent.Properties)
    {
       if (ContainsBlacklistedData(prop))
         logEvent.AddOrUpdateProperty(RewriteWithoutIt(prop));
    }
  }
}
```

This way, Serilog's built-in destructuring does all the work; all you need to implement is a recursive filter.

