Hi Pär,

Awaiting is needed if you want to flow the contextual properties. Otherwise, there's nothing actually linking the original and new execution contexts once ProcessAsync() has returned.

LogContext properties will flow even if the callee is using a different ForContext()-created ILogger, so long as they're attached to the same pipeline.

If you just want to avoid an await in the message handling loop, you could do:

``` csharp
subscriber.SubscribeAsync<BasicMessage>((message, context) =>
{
    var msgId = context.GlobalMessageId; //passed between handlers
    return Task.Run(async () =>
    {
        using (LogContext.PushProperty("GlobalMessageId", msgId))
        {
            return await _businessLogic.ProcessAsync(message.Prop); // call to underlying business layer
        }
    }
});
```

(Forgive the inevitable syntax errors here :-))

